<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brihatkatha</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Brihatkatha, 'Great Story', was a vast collection of tales attributed to the poet Gunadhya and written in Paishachi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a>, traditionally at a Satavahana court. The original is lost, but it is known through later retellings, including the Kathasaritsagara of Somadeva and the Brihatkathamanjari of Kshemendra, both from eleventh-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. It was a major source of Indian story literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brihatkatha.jpg" alt="Brihatkatha" width="320" height="151" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Phil wink · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brhatkatha_stemma.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The date of the work is uncertain. Gunadhya may have flourished under a Satavahana king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paithan">Pratishthana</a>, the modern Paithan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>; according to D. C. Sircar he probably lived between the 1st century BCE and the 3rd century CE. Another account, in the Nepala Mahatmya of the Skanda Purana, makes him a native of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> and court poet of King Madana of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, a tradition Sircar calls less authentic. Paishachi, the language he used, is a little-known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a>, described as the speech of the common people of the border regions of northwest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>The earliest surviving reference seems to be that of Subandhu in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasavadatta">Vasavadatta</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banabhatta">Banabhatta</a> refers to the work in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harshacharita">Harshacharita</a> and the Kadambari. Dandin, in the Kavyadarsha, calls it marvellous and says it was composed in the vernacular of the bhutas, though his information appears to be second-hand. A Cambodian inscription of about 875 names Gunadhya, and the Kannada Kavirajamarga of about 850 mentions a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> version, now lost, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durvinita">Durvinita</a>. A romantic work by Gunadhya can safely be assumed to have existed before 600 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The adaptations differ too much for the original to be reconstructed whole, but what they share allows strong inferences. The life of Udayana of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kausambi">Kaushambi</a> served as a prelude: his upbringing in a hermitage after a monstrous bird carried off his pregnant mother, his captivity under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pradyota">Pradyota</a>, king of Ujjayini, his elopement with Pradyota's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasavadatta">Vasavadatta</a>, and his second marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pawaya">Padmavati</a>, arranged by ministers who made him believe Vasavadatta dead. The central story was that of their son Naravahanadatta, born as a boon of Kubera and fated to become emperor of the Vidyadharas. Kshemendra's version is in eighteen books called lambakas, as the original is thought to have been.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Besides the two Kashmiri versions, the story is known from the Sanskrit Brihatkathashlokasamgraha of Budhasvamin, of which the complete text has not been found, and from the Perunkatai and the Vasudevahimdi in vernacular languages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dandin">Dandin</a> states that the original was in prose, not in the verse of the Kashmiri recensions. Scholars compare Gunadhya with Vyasa and Valmiki, and the disappearance of his text is counted a very great loss to Indian literature.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brihatkatha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prakrit literature flourished: tradition attributes to King Hala the Gatha Saptashati, an anthology of seven hundred short poems of love and village life that shaped later Indian poetics, and places the lost <b>Brihatkatha</b> of Gunadhya, source of later story collections, at a Satavahana court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
